UID:10012322-1787490000-1787495400@njaudubon.org
SUMMARY:NJ Audubon: Creating Wildlife Habitats in Urban Homes
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Anthony Contreras\, NJ Audubon Summer Intern & Brown University Graduate Student \nTurn your bricks\, balconies\, and windows into a beautiful oasis for native plants\, pollinators\, birds\, and for yourself using sustainable practices. Creating and maintaining habitats for native species in urban areas is vital for the conservation of our beloved animals. In a forever urbanizing and developing world there is plenty you can create and garden for even in your urban home. In this one-hour presentation and half hour garden tour we will be reviewing methods on maximizing the little space you have available to create wildlife gardens and habitats. This is perfect for individuals and families that live in apartments\, townhouses\, and condos\, or for those who want to create a microhabitat. \nTicket sale ends 08/22 at 3:00 PM.  \nCOST: FREE \nInterested in becoming a member? Please click on the following link to learn more or join: Become a Member today! \nCANCELLATION: NJ Audubon’s programs require a minimum number of registrants to run. If we cancel\, due to lack of participation\, severe weather\, or unforeseen circumstances\, all participants will be notified as soon as possible and are eligible for a full refund. Participants may cancel without penalty if greater than 48 hours in advance. Sorry\, no refund for cancellations less than 48 hours in advance unless due to health emergencies; however\, registrations can be transferred to another program in the future. \nREGISTER HERE \n 
URL:https://njaudubon.org/event/nj-audubon-creating-wildlife-habitats-in-urban-homes/
LOCATION:Scherman Hoffman Wildlife Sanctuary\, 11 Hardscrabble Rd\, Bernardsville\, NJ\, 07924\, United States
CATEGORIES:Lecture,Nature Walk,Scherman Hoffman Wildlife Sanctuary,Workshop
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://njaudubon.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/NJ-Audubon-Creating-Wildlife-Habitats-in-Urban-Homes.jpeg
GEO:40.7448693;-74.5523161
